virglrenderer: flush a fence's GL work only when something needs it

What: vrend_renderer_create_fence() no longer calls glFlush() for every fence.
While the same GL context goes on with its next command buffer, the flush is
held, and done before any other GL context is made current or the context is
destroyed (the hop to vrend's own context that QEMU makes before every command
excepted), when QEMU asks (new virgl_renderer_flush_fences(): before every
command that is not a command buffer, after the last one of its queue), at the
start of every renderer call that may read guest work outside a GL command
buffer (poll, transfers, resource create/unref/map/info, scanout, cursor, a
Venus command buffer, a wait on another context's fence), and at the next fence
once the oldest held one is OMACVM_VIRGL_FENCE_FLUSH_US old (default 2000).
vrend's GL context callbacks are wrapped for this (render thread only).
OMACVM_VIRGL_FENCE_FLUSH=0 flushes every fence at once again;
OMACVM_VIRGL_FENCE_FLUSH_STATS=1 logs the counts every 10 s.
Why: every guest command buffer comes with a fence, and Mesa sends one each
time its 64K-dword buffer is full: WebGL Aquarium at 30k fish sends ~41 a
frame. Each glFlush ends Apple's Metal render pass and commits a command
buffer: vrend_renderer_create_fence was ~6 % of the render thread (mini, M4).
The guest learns of a fence only between QEMU's commands, so a fence held while
QEMU still has commands to run delays nothing the guest sees.
Upstream: not sent (depends on QEMU's batch hook, Apple GL behaviour).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-fence-flush.c (no GL: every place a held fence must be
flushed, in which context, and that nothing else flushes); test-fence-flush-gl.c
(the public API the way QEMU drives it, on the Mac's GL with the sync thread: on
an M4 Max a fence never flushed does not signal, and without the flush on a
context switch a copy in another context reads the old colour; both are cases);
run-regressions.py check_fence_flush_entries (the renderer calls that flush).

